From: Waltteri Hooli <wahooli@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] ASoC: qcom: common: follow the DAI components the card resolved

Restart the audio DSP and the sound card never comes back, even once every
codec, SoundWire master and slave underneath it has rebound:

    snd-x1e80100 sound: ASoC: CODEC DAI  not registered
    # cat /proc/asound/cards
    --- no soundcards ---

The DAI names the card resolved at probe are pointers borrowed from the
components -- snd_soc_dai_name_get() returns dai->driver->name, it does not
copy -- and nothing records that the card depends on those components staying
put. So when the components are unbound the card is left bound, registered on
ASoC's rebind list, still holding the old pointers, and every retry compares
against names that no longer exist. The name prints empty above because that
is all that is left of it.

Unbinding and rebinding the card by hand fixes it, because a fresh probe
re-parses the tree and resolves the names again. Nothing triggers that on its
own.

Record the dependency instead. A managed device link gets both halves: the
card is unbound along with its components, while they are still healthy, and
DL_FLAG_AUTOPROBE_CONSUMER has the core probe it again once they are back.

Components whose device is destroyed rather than unbound -- the DSP's own GPR
services -- take their link with them, but the LPASS macros are ordinary
platform devices that outlive the DSP, so rebinding one of those brings the
card back. Non-platform components such as SoundWire slaves have no platform
device to link to and are skipped; they come and go with a master that is
itself linked.

This is the last hop of a dependency chain that reaches from the DSP's clock
controller down to the card, none of which was previously expressed.

---
--- a/sound/soc/qcom/common.c
+++ b/sound/soc/qcom/common.c
@@ -4,6 +4,7 @@
 
 #include <dt-bindings/sound/qcom,q6afe.h>
 #include <linux/module.h>
+#include <linux/of_platform.h>
 #include <sound/jack.h>
 #include <linux/input-event-codes.h>
 #include "common.h"
@@ -23,6 +24,51 @@
 	SND_SOC_DAPM_SPK("DP7 Jack", NULL),
 };
 
+/*
+ * Depend on the device behind a resolved DAI, so the card follows it.
+ *
+ * The card holds the DAI names it resolved at probe, and those are pointers
+ * borrowed from the components -- snd_soc_dai_name_get() hands back
+ * dai->driver->name, it does not copy. Nothing records that the card needs
+ * those components to stay put, so when one is unbound (an audio DSP restart
+ * takes the whole LPASS stack down with it) the card is left registered on
+ * ASoC's rebind list still holding the old pointers. It then retries forever
+ * against names that no longer exist:
+ *
+ *   snd-x1e80100 sound: ASoC: CODEC DAI  not registered
+ *
+ * with the name printed empty, because that is what is left of it. Only a
+ * fresh probe re-parses the tree and re-resolves the names.
+ *
+ * A managed link gets both halves for free: the card is unbound with its
+ * components while they are still healthy, and DL_FLAG_AUTOPROBE_CONSUMER
+ * probes it again once they are back. Components whose device is destroyed
+ * rather than unbound (the DSP's own services) take their link with them, but
+ * the LPASS macros are ordinary platform devices that outlive the DSP, and
+ * rebinding one of those is enough to bring the card back.
+ *
+ * Non-platform components -- SoundWire slaves, say -- have no platform device
+ * to link to and are skipped; they come and go with a master that is itself
+ * linked.
+ */
+static void qcom_snd_link_dai_device(struct device *card_dev,
+				     struct device_node *np)
+{
+	struct platform_device *pdev;
+
+	if (!np)
+		return;
+
+	pdev = of_find_device_by_node(np);
+	if (!pdev)
+		return;
+
+	if (&pdev->dev != card_dev)
+		device_link_add(card_dev, &pdev->dev, DL_FLAG_AUTOPROBE_CONSUMER);
+
+	put_device(&pdev->dev);
+}
+
 int qcom_snd_parse_of(struct snd_soc_card *card)
 {
 	struct device *dev = card->dev;
@@ -159,6 +205,11 @@
 			link->nonatomic = 1;
 		}
 
+		qcom_snd_link_dai_device(dev, link->cpus->of_node);
+		qcom_snd_link_dai_device(dev, link->platforms->of_node);
+		for (int i = 0; i < link->num_codecs; i++)
+			qcom_snd_link_dai_device(dev, link->codecs[i].of_node);
+
 		link->stream_name = link->name;
 		link++;
 	}
